@@ -119,10 +119,25 @@ ### Call the next ticket
 Return called ticket / display payload
 ```
 
-The implementation must prevent two concurrent attendants from successfully
-calling the same waiting ticket. The exact locking strategy belongs to the
-implementation and database transaction layer; the domain invariant is that a
-ticket can be called only once.
+The domain step is `App\Actions\CallNextTicket`
+(`__invoke(Queue, User, CallMode = Any): Ticket`). It does not authorize: the
+caller is trusted and the HTTP/Livewire layer will. Inside one transaction it:
+
+1. locks the queue row and reads `is_active` from the database
+   (`InactiveQueueException` if inactive, nothing changes);
+2. picks the best waiting candidate for the mode (priority desc, `issued_at`
+   asc, `id` asc; fairness applied in `any` mode only, see
+   [`business-rules.md`](business-rules.md#ordering-and-fairness));
+3. locks the candidate row and re-checks it is still `waiting`, because
+   `Ticket::call()` does not lock the queue; if it was called meanwhile it is
+   skipped and the next candidate is picked;
+4. sets `status`, `called_at` and `called_by_user_id` (the only mutable
+   fields) and returns the updated ticket;
+5. throws `NoWaitingTicketsException` when no ticket is eligible.
+
+Two concurrent attendants therefore cannot both call the same waiting ticket:
+the domain invariant is that a ticket can be called only once.
+(`lockForUpdate()` is a no-op on SQLite, which only the test suite uses.)
 
 > TODO (follow-up from review): link the test covering the single-call
 > invariant and concurrent-call protection once implemented.
